// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma hyperion >=0.0.1;

/**
 * The epoch clock every Ark Staking contract shares.
 *
 * The one rule this library exists to enforce: **every contract in this product
 * derives its epoch from the same expression**, so a delivery, a coupon, a
 * release bound and a concentration window cannot disagree about which epoch
 * they are in. A per-contract epoch parameter would let a deployment set two of
 * them differently, and the failure would be silent — value credited to an
 * epoch nothing else believes exists.
 *
 * A CONSTANT, WHERE THE WHITEPAPER MAKES IT GOVERNABLE
 * ----------------------------------------------------
 * Whitepaper §6.3 carries an epoch of seven days, governable, bounded only by
 * the coupon cliff. It is a `constant` here, and that is a deliberate
 * departure. An epoch number is the key a delivery, a coupon draw, a spent
 * proof and an accepted root are all recorded under, so a vote that changed
 * the length would not adjust a rate: it would repoint every one of those
 * records at a different bucket, on two chains at once. The whitepaper's bound
 * cannot rescue that, because it constrains the new value and says nothing
 * about the records already written under the old one.
 *
 * `COUPON_CLIFF` is carried here rather than in `ArkCatBond` so the bound is
 * checkable against the value it constrains from one place. Both are
 * `constant`, so `EPOCH_LENGTH <= COUPON_CLIFF` holds by inspection; the suite
 * asserts it anyway, because the two would otherwise be free to drift apart in
 * separate commits.
 *
 * Epochs are numbered from the Unix epoch rather than from a genesis timestamp,
 * so two contracts deployed at different moments still agree without being
 * configured to.
 */
library ArkEpoch {
    /// The value of whitepaper §6.3.
    uint64 internal constant EPOCH_LENGTH = 7 days;

    /**
     * The interval from issuance in which a bond accrues but does not pay.
     *
     * A constant of whitepaper §6.4, and the upper bound §6.3 places on the
     * epoch length: below one epoch cycle the cliff does not bind at all, so an
     * epoch longer than the cliff would let a subscription made immediately
     * before a determination collect a coupon and exit — which is the single
     * thing the cliff exists to prevent.
     */
    uint64 internal constant COUPON_CLIFF = 30 days;

    /**
     * From a release instruction to the earliest settlement. Whitepaper §6.4.
     *
     * Here rather than in the registry because two contracts check relations
     * against it — the registry's transit record and the exit parity and
     * run-off drain of `ArkStakeParams` — and a second declaration would be a
     * second value. It exceeds the dispute cycle with its appeal, twenty days,
     * which is what stops a release from outrunning a determination already
     * open against the position releasing.
     */
    uint64 internal constant UNBONDING_PERIOD = 21 days;

    /**
     * The four windows of the premium swap (whitepaper §4.7, §6.3), from the
     * epoch after the one whose premium is offered. `ecdsa-stakings/shared/
     * contracts/ArkEpochLib.sol` carries the two the escrow reads.
     *
     *   quote window        solvers quote lots, closing with epoch t + 1
     *   admission window    the root carrying the selected locks is admitted
     *   origin claim window the escrow pays a lot against its preimage
     *   QRL claim margin    the preimage published there is presented here
     *
     * Constants, where whitepaper §6.3 makes them governable within ranges, for
     * the reason `EPOCH_LENGTH` is (CLAUDE.md §7.10): each is a boundary a
     * lot's expiry, a credit and a coupon draw are recorded against, so a vote
     * would repoint records rather than adjust a rate. The values are the
     * specification's and satisfy both of its relations by inspection: the
     * margin is not below the admission and claim windows together (I31), and
     * the four together are below the epoch, so the settlement of epoch t
     * closes before the quote window offering t + 1 opens.
     */
    uint64 internal constant QUOTE_WINDOW = 1 days;
    uint64 internal constant ADMISSION_WINDOW = 1 days;
    uint64 internal constant EVM_CLAIM_WINDOW = 1 days;
    /// The specification's value. The testnet profile raises it to twice Hoodi's finality plus a relayer interval (D12): `scripts/lib/profile.js`.
    uint64 internal constant QRL_CLAIM_MARGIN = 2 days;

    /**
     * The root-level challenge window of the attestation contract (whitepaper
     * §3.5, yellow paper §1.3): seven days in which any observer can re-derive
     * and disprove what the attester quorum sealed. Anything the quorum reports
     * — a delegation reconciliation among them — is acted on only after it.
     */
    uint64 internal constant CHALLENGE_WINDOW = 7 days;

    /**
     * The dispute cycle with its appeal, which is the longest a maturity may be
     * held against a claim (whitepaper §5.3, §6.4).
     *
     * Here rather than in the bond for `UNBONDING_PERIOD`'s reason: the comment
     * on that constant already cites this figure, and two contracts now check a
     * relation against it, so a second declaration would be a second value. A
     * bare duration literal and not an expression over another constant, because
     * `test/profile.test.js` reads it as one.
     */
    uint64 internal constant DISPUTE_CYCLE_APPEAL = 20 days;

    /// When the quote window offering `epoch`'s premium closes: the end of `epoch + 1`.
    function quoteCloses(uint256 epoch) internal pure returns (uint256) {
        return (epoch + 2) * EPOCH_LENGTH;
    }

    /// When it opens.
    function quoteOpens(uint256 epoch) internal pure returns (uint256) {
        return quoteCloses(epoch) - QUOTE_WINDOW;
    }

    /// The last instant, exclusive, the escrow pays a lot of `epoch`'s offering.
    function evmExpiry(uint256 epoch) internal pure returns (uint256) {
        return quoteCloses(epoch) + ADMISSION_WINDOW + EVM_CLAIM_WINDOW;
    }

    /**
     * The QRL expiry of the same lots, which closes the settlement window of
     * `epoch`: its proceeds are final from here and its coupon is paid, early
     * in `epoch + 2` (whitepaper §4.7).
     */
    function settlementCloses(uint256 epoch) internal pure returns (uint256) {
        return evmExpiry(epoch) + QRL_CLAIM_MARGIN;
    }

    /// The epoch a timestamp falls in.
    function epochOf(uint256 timestamp) internal pure returns (uint256) {
        return timestamp / EPOCH_LENGTH;
    }

    /// The epoch in progress.
    function current() internal view returns (uint256) {
        return block.timestamp / EPOCH_LENGTH;
    }

    /// The first instant of an epoch, for a bound that must be checked against it.
    function startOf(uint256 epoch) internal pure returns (uint256) {
        return epoch * EPOCH_LENGTH;
    }
}
